WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.487
- There's been a lot of press coverage

00:00:01.487 --> 00:00:03.970
about people quitting their jobs.

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.580
Well, today's report tells you why.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.070
- [Josephine] Here, President Biden

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:11.340
is referring to the Labor
Department's monthly jobs report,

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.567
a key gauge of American
employment and inflation.

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:17.639
The report can move trillions
of dollars in market trades

00:00:17.639 --> 00:00:20.020
and influence policy decisions,

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.430
like interest rates at
the Federal Reserve.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.780
But the report still has its limits.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:25.620
- There are some things

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.540
that the jobs report just doesn't include.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.420
And because of that,

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:32.190
we're not getting a complete
picture of the US jobs market.

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:33.163
- [Josephine] Here's what you need to know

00:00:33.163 --> 00:00:35.440
to read the monthly jobs report,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.913
including what it tells
you, and what it doesn't.

00:00:41.194 --> 00:00:42.953
The report, which is normally released

00:00:42.953 --> 00:00:45.210
on the first Friday of the month,

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.598
gives a look at the current
state of the economy.

00:00:47.598 --> 00:00:50.130
And while economists read it in depth,

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.004
there are four key areas
that they tend to focus on.

00:00:53.004 --> 00:00:56.800
Non-farm payrolls, often
simply referred to as jobs,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.460
shows the number of positions added

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:02.260
or lost each month that
aren't in agriculture.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.720
This chart shows non-farm
payrolls over the past decade.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.269
You can see job numbers
have been steadily rising

00:01:08.269 --> 00:01:11.211
until the pandemic hit
when they plummeted.

00:01:11.211 --> 00:01:13.521
And although jobs had started to recover,

00:01:13.521 --> 00:01:16.890
by late 2021 job growth had slowed.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:17.723
- Weak job growth

00:01:17.723 --> 00:01:21.075
can be a reflection of
two different things:

00:01:21.075 --> 00:01:25.910
one is that businesses are
struggling to find workers,

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:28.540
which is largely what we saw in 2021.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:29.529
- [Josephine] Sarah Chaney Cambon

00:01:29.529 --> 00:01:31.417
reports on the American labor market

00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:33.030
for The Wall Street Journal.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.300
- The other is that demand

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.550
for their goods and services is weak.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:40.957
And therefore, they're waiting
off workers or not hiring.

00:01:40.957 --> 00:01:43.010
- [Josephine] This fluctuating jobs number

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.200
is directly tied to the unemployment rate,

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.690
which measures how many workers

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:48.510
in the labor force are unemployed

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.020
and currently looking for work.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.948
The goal is full employment,
which some economists say

00:01:52.948 --> 00:01:56.610
is around a 4% to 5% unemployment rate.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.290
- Full employment is basically

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.590
when anyone who wants a job can get a job.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.160
When the unemployment rate is really low,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.130
it's generally a good thing.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.528
It means that businesses
are not laying off workers

00:02:10.528 --> 00:02:13.459
and wages are probably
growing pretty strongly

00:02:13.459 --> 00:02:16.070
as a result of low joblessness.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:17.964
- [Josephine] But if
unemployment drops too low,

00:02:17.964 --> 00:02:20.060
it can signal challenges.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.150
- The downside of low unemployment

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.540
can be that companies are really
struggling to find workers

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.420
and that can lead to them raising wages,

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:32.275
and therefore prices,

00:02:32.275 --> 00:02:36.460
and you could get very big
inflationary pressures.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.420
- [Josephine] When the
unemployment rate goes down,

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.430
economists look into how much of that

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.340
is due to workers getting jobs

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:45.770
and how much is due to shrinking
labor force participation,

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.920
which is measured by the
labor force participation rate

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.170
or how much of the overall population

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:54.400
is either currently working
or looking for a job.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.060
You can see labor force
participation has steadily declined

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:00.760
over the past two decades
with the largest drop

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.710
happening at the
beginning of the pandemic.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:04.117
And when the rate drops too low,

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:06.370
it can put pressure on employers.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:10.640
- Generally when the labor
force participation rate is low

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.200
as it's been throughout the pandemic,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.320
you might see stronger wage growth

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.784
and that's because businesses
just can't find the workers

00:03:18.784 --> 00:03:20.100
to fill their jobs.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.310
They're oftentimes facing
what we call labor shortages.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.900
And as a result, they ramp up pay.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.422
- [Josephine] Increasing or decreasing pay

00:03:28.422 --> 00:03:31.170
is measured by wage growth.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.390
Look at this chart.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:35.060
You'll notice that wages picked up in 2021

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.550
due to labor shortages

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:40.440
and economists expect that
wages will continue to rise.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:41.800
Metrics like wage growth

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:44.970
give economists a sense of
how the economy is doing

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.450
but the data only shows
part of the picture.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.470
That's because the report
is mainly just hard data,

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.114
which means it can tell
us whether job numbers

00:03:52.114 --> 00:03:56.240
and wages are going up or down
but it doesn't explain why.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:57.529
- [Josephine] It doesn't give us details

00:03:57.529 --> 00:03:59.850
on how good a given job is.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.989
It doesn't tell us how
safe a manufacturing job is

00:04:02.989 --> 00:04:07.129
or how well an employer is
treating their employees.

00:04:07.129 --> 00:04:09.399
- [Josephine] And there's
also the timing of the report.

00:04:09.399 --> 00:04:12.902
It's based on two core surveys
from the Labor Department.

00:04:12.902 --> 00:04:16.072
One of household populations
and another of employment data

00:04:16.072 --> 00:04:19.100
that take place around
the 12th of every month.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:20.750
And as a result, the jobs report

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:22.423
primarily reflects the labor market

00:04:22.423 --> 00:04:24.750
over these first two weeks,

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.250
which means there's a delay in the data.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.950
The employment survey collects data

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:32.090
from around 145,000 employers.

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:35.720
Some of whom may respond to
the survey late or not at all.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:39.100
- Before the pandemic
hit businesses were open.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:42.680
And so, they were able
able to provide responses

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:44.570
to the Labor Department survey.

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:47.938
After the pandemic hit, a
lot of businesses shut down

00:04:47.938 --> 00:04:51.350
either because consumers stopped going

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.100
to X restaurant or X business,

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:57.370
or because of
government-mandated restrictions.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:04:59.000
- [Josephine] If businesses don't respond,

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.670
the Labor Department has to
guess their payroll size,

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:02.980
which can get tricky.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:05.620
And this is what we saw
happening during the pandemic.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.350
- The Labor Department
had a smaller sample size

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:11.610
of businesses to work with
for its payroll survey.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.856
And as a result, the margin of error

00:05:13.856 --> 00:05:16.570
when they were making
calculations was higher.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:18.690
- [Josephine] This margin
of error is important

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:22.530
in part because of just how
big the labor market is.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:27.350
In December 2021, it stood
at almost 150 million jobs.

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:29.088
If the number of jobs added or lost

00:05:29.088 --> 00:05:31.530
are tens of thousands higher or lower

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:32.860
than what the report shows,

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:35.140
it could make policymaker's jobs harder.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:38.260
- The Federal Reserve is
really closely watching

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.210
those job growth and
payrolls growth numbers.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.169
And if there's the big margin of error,

00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:46.490
they might have a slight
misunderstanding what's going on.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.310
- [Josephine] Still, the Fed
looks at multiple data reports

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:51.138
about the economy and over time,

00:05:51.138 --> 00:05:53.030
estimates from the jobs report

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:55.640
that are too high or low tend to even out,

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:57.670
so the implications are shorter term.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:05:59.907
But despite the pitfalls, the jobs report

00:05:59.907 --> 00:06:03.550
is still one of the most
influential indicators we have

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:06.053
for the American workforce and economy.

